The price-drift audit now checks whether yoyo can price a model by asking the same price lookup /cost uses, not the hard-coded list of known models. All four models from #1003 are now compared, and the change is committed as 611138da. Clippy, cargo fmt --check and the full cargo test suite are green.

What changed (src/format/cost/price_audit_tests.rs):
• `yoyo_id_for now asks price_of whether an id can be priced, instead of looking it up in known_models_for_provider. I didn't write a second matcher, and src/providers.rs` didn't need any change. The provider must still be one the audit reads, and the Gemini spelling aliases still apply.
• New offline test the_audit_asks_the_pricing_resolver_not_the_shipped_list:
  • `claude-opus-5-5, claude-fable-5-1, claude-haiku-5-5` and a dated Haiku id count as priceable and get compared; a 3× price change on each is reported as drift.
  • It first checks that these ids really are missing from the known-models list, so it can't pass trivially.
  • Near misses: acme-imaginary-9 stays "cannot price", and the same Opus id under openrouter stays a coverage gap.
• **One existing test expectation changed:** two old tests used `deepseek-v4-flash-vision-exp` as their example of "yoyo can't price this". `/cost` does price it, on purpose, so the test now expects it to be compared, with an in-place note saying the old expectation is superseded. deepseek-imaginary-9 replaces it as the unpriceable example. The repo's assertion-weakening checker found nothing it would flag; it listed this one as "could not judge".
• **Check that the test can fail:** putting the old list lookup back (marked `NEUTERED`), running the tests and restoring the file, all in one command, made the new test and the mapping test fail by name. After the restore all 19 tests passed and the marker was gone.

Live audit against models.dev (182 rows):

| | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| compared | 36 | 112 |
| drifted | 9 | 75 |
| cannot price | 146 | 70 |

• Opus 5.5, Fable 5.1 and Haiku 5.5 are now compared and their prices match.
• **The drift alarm got much louder (66 new rows).** Most are models no provider list includes, priced by loose name matching. For example, `gpt-5.4-pro` is billed at gpt-5's $2/$8 against $30/$180 on models.dev. Those are the numbers /cost really shows, so they look like real wrong prices, not noise. I didn't patch them; that needs the vendors' own pricing pages.
• **Sonnet 5.5 didn't come out the way the plan said.** It is compared, but it isn't reported as drift. Our cache-read price is 0.2 where models.dev has 0.1, and the audit files it under "only the cache-read price differs". That category is meant for rows where we left the cache-read price at 0.0, but the code never checks that. The per-row table prints DRIFT for the same row, so the report contradicts itself. I filed this as #1004 rather than fixing it here.

I also marked the old "only models yoyo ships are compared" claim in ARCHITECTURE.md as superseded, with these numbers next to it. The commit says "Part of #1003", not "Fixes", because Sonnet 5.5's price is still unresolved. Still open, and a separate product decision: whether to add the 5.5 models to the known-models list used by /model list and completions.
